-- result = true
Example: Querying against an xml type column



If an expression returns a sequence of nodes, you may have to find the type of the node in the
sequence. The following examples illustrate how sequence type syntax can be used to evaluate
the node type returned by an expression. You can use the following sequence types:
item() - Matches any item in the sequence.
node() - Determines whether the sequence is a node.
processing-instruction() - Determines whether the expression returns a processing
instruction.
comment() - Determines whether the expression returns a comment.
document-node() - Determines whether the expression returns a document node.
The following example illustrates these sequence types.
In this example, several queries are executed against an untyped XML variable. These queries
illustrate the use of sequence types.
```sql
SELECT Instructions.query(''
```

declare namespace AWMI="https://schemas.microsoft.com/sqlserver/2004/07/adventure-
works/ProductModelManuInstructions";
data(/AWMI:root[1]/AWMI:Location[1]/@LocationID) instance of xs:integer?'') as
Result
FROM Production.ProductModel
WHERE ProductModelID = 7
Comparing the Node Type Returned by an
Expression
Example: Using sequence types
DECLARE @var XML
SET @var = ''<?xml-stylesheet href="someValue" type="text/xsl" ?>
<root>text node

  <a>Data a</a>

</root>''



In the first query, the expression returns the typed value of element <a >. In the second query,
the expression returns element <a >. Both are items. Therefore, both queries return True.
All the XQuery expressions in the following three queries return the element node child of the
<root > element. Therefore, the sequence type expression, instance of node() , returns True,
and the other two expressions, instance of text()  and instance of document-node() , return
False.
In the following query, the instance of document-node()  expression returns True, because the
parent of the <root > element is a document node.
In the following query, the expression retrieves the first node from the XML instance. Because it
is a processing instruction node, the instance of processing-instruction()  expression returns
True.
These are the specific limitations:
document-node() with content type syntax is not supported.
processing-instruction(name) syntax is not supported.
```sql
SELECT @var.query(''data(/root[1]/a[1]) instance of item()'')
SELECT @var.query(''/root[1]/a[1] instance of item()'')
SELECT @var.query(''(/root/*)[1] instance of node()'')
SELECT @var.query(''(/root/*)[1] instance of text()'')
SELECT @var.query(''(/root/*)[1] instance of document-node()'')
SELECT @var.query(''(/root/..)[1] instance of document-node()'') -- true
SELECT @var.query(''(/node())[1] instance of processing-instruction()'')
```

Implementation Limitations



An element test is used to match the element node returned by an expression to an element
node with a specific name and type. You can use these element tests:
The attribute test determines whether the attribute returned by an expression is an attribute
node. You can use these attribute tests.
attribute()
attribute(AttributeName)
attribute(AttributeName, AttributeType)
The following examples illustrate scenarios in which element and attribute tests are useful.
The following XML schema defines the CustomerType  complex type where <firstName > and
<lastName > elements are optional. For a specified XML instance, you may have to determine
whether the first name exists for a particular customer.
Element Tests
element ()
element(ElementName)
element(ElementName, ElementType?)
element(*, ElementType?)
