VALUES (
    'chunk_ec2636415f22',
    'converted_sql-sql-server-ver17.md',
    57124,
    '### SQL

Another example of implicit atomization is when you use arithmetic operators. The +  operator
requires atomic values, and data()  is implicitly applied to retrieve the atomic value of the
LaborHours  attribute. The query is specified against the Instructions column of the xml type in
the ProductModel table. The following query returns the LaborHours  attribute three times. In
the query, consider:
In constructing the OriginalLaborHours  attribute, atomization is implicitly applied to the
singleton sequence returned by $WC/@LaborHours . The typed value of the LaborHours
attribute is assigned to OriginalLaborHours .
DECLARE @x AS XML;
SET @x = ''<ROOT><Location LID="1" SetupTime="1.1" LaborHours="3.3" />
<Location LID="2" SetupTime="1.0" LaborHours="5" />
<Location LID="3" SetupTime="2.1" LaborHours="4" />',
