  <Color>Red</Color>
  <Weight>25</Weight>
  <Warranty>2 years parts and labor</Warranty>
</Features></ProductModel>'');
<ProductModel ProductModelID="111">
  This is product model catalog description.
  <Summary>Some description</Summary>
  <Features>
    <Color>Red</Color>
    <Weight>25</Weight>
    <Warranty>2 years parts and labor</Warranty>
  </Features>
</ProductModel>
DECLARE @x AS XML;
SET @x = ''<root>5</root>'';
```sql
SELECT @x.query(''<NewRoot><e> { /root } </e></NewRoot>'');
```

<NewRoot>
  <e>
    <root>5</root>



The following query is similar to the previous one. However, the expression in the curly braces
specifies the data()  function to retrieve the atomic value of the <root>  element and assigns it
to the constructed element, <e> .',
